# 6. Database Design

The Online Book Store uses SQLite for data persistence, with a relational database schema managed by the [DatabaseManager](file:///c:/Users/Ahmed/IdeaProjects/Online-Book-Store/src/main/java/com/example/onlinebookstore/Models/DatabaseManager.java#11-111) Singleton pattern.

---

## 6.1 Database Overview

**Database Type**: SQLite  
**File**: [bookstore.db](file:///c:/Users/Ahmed/IdeaProjects/Online-Book-Store/bookstore.db)  
**Total Tables**: 6  
**Management**: [DatabaseManager.java](file:///c:/Users/Ahmed/IdeaProjects/Online-Book-Store/src/main/java/com/example/onlinebookstore/Models/DatabaseManager.java) (Singleton)  
**Access**: JDBC

### Why SQLite?

- **Embedded**: No separate database server required
- **Lightweight**:  Small footprint, perfect for desktop applications
- **Self-contained**: Single file database
- **ACID Compliant**: Ensures data integrity
- **Zero Configuration**: No setup or administration needed

---

## 6.2 Database Schema

All tables are automatically created by `DatabaseManager.createTables()` method on first run.

### 6.2.1 Users Table

Stores both Customer and Admin users.

```sql
CREATE TABLE users (
    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,
    username TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL,
    password TEXT NOT NULL,
    address TEXT,
    phone TEXT,
    role TEXT NOT NULL  -- 'CUSTOMER' or 'ADMIN'
);
```

**Columns**:

| Column | Type | Constraints | Description |
|--------|------|-------------|-------------|
| `id` | INTEGER | PRIMARY KEY, AUTOINCREMENT | Unique user identifier |
| `username` | TEXT | UNIQUE, NOT NULL | Login username |
| `password` | TEXT | NOT NULL | User password (plaintext - should be hashed) |
| `address` | TEXT | NULL | Customer address (NULL for admins) |
| `phone` | TEXT | NULL | Customer phone (NULL for admins) |
| `role` | TEXT | NOT NULL | "CUSTOMER" or "ADMIN" |

**Purpose**: Stores all user accounts. The `role` field determines user type, used by [UserFactory](file:///c:/Users/Ahmed/IdeaProjects/Online-Book-Store/src/main/java/com/example/onlinebookstore/Models/UserFactory.java#3-14) to create appropriate objects.

---

### 6.2.2 Categories Table

Stores book categories for classification and filtering.

```sql
CREATE TABLE categories (
    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,
    name TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL,
    description TEXT
);
```

**Columns**:

| Column | Type | Constraints | Description |
|--------|------|-------------|-------------|
| `id` | INTEGER | PRIMARY KEY, AUTOINCREMENT | Unique category identifier |
| [name](file:///c:/Users/Ahmed/IdeaProjects/Online-Book-Store/src/main/java/com/example/onlinebookstore/Models/User.java#20-21) | TEXT | UNIQUE, NOT NULL | Category name (e.g., "Fiction", "Technology") |
| `description` | TEXT | NULL | Optional description |

**Purpose**: Organizes books into categories. Admins can create, edit, and delete categories.

**Sample Categories**:
- Fiction
- Non-Fiction
- Science
- Technology
- History

---

### 6.2.3 Books Table

Stores all book information including inventory details.

```sql
CREATE TABLE books (
    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,
    title TEXT NOT NULL,
    author TEXT NOT NULL,
    price REAL NOT NULL,
    stock INTEGER NOT NULL,
    category TEXT,
    popularity INTEGER DEFAULT 0,
    edition TEXT,
    coverImage TEXT
);
```

**Columns**:

| Column | Type | Constraints | Description |
|--------|------|-------------|-------------|
| `id` | INTEGER | PRIMARY KEY, AUTOINCREMENT | Unique book identifier |
| `title` | TEXT | NOT NULL | Book title |
| `author` | TEXT | NOT NULL | Book author |
| `price` | REAL | NOT NULL | Book price in currency units |
| `stock` | INTEGER | NOT NULL | Available quantity in inventory |
| `category` | TEXT | NULL | Category name (should reference categories table) |
| `popularity` | INTEGER | DEFAULT 0 | Sales count or popularity metric |
| `edition` | TEXT | NULL | Book edition (e.g., "1st Edition", "4th Edition") |
| `coverImage` | TEXT | NULL | URL to cover image (e.g., OpenLibrary URL) |

**Purpose**: Central repository for all book data. Updated by Admins and queried by Customers.

**Note**: `category` stores category name as TEXT. Ideally, this should be a foreign key to `categories.id` for better normalization.

**Example Book Data**:
- "Clean Code" by Robert C. Martin - Technology, $42.99
- "The Great Gatsby" by F. Scott Fitzgerald - Fiction, $10.99
- "Design Patterns" by Gang of Four - Technology, $44.99

---

### 6.2.4 Orders Table

Stores customer orders with status tracking.

```sql
CREATE TABLE orders (
    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,
    customerId INTEGER NOT NULL,
    orderDate TEXT NOT NULL,
    status TEXT NOT NULL,  -- 'Pending', 'Confirmed', 'Shipped', 'Delivered', 'Canceled'
    totalPrice REAL NOT NULL,
    FOREIGN KEY (customerId) REFERENCES users(id)
);
```

**Columns**:

| Column | Type | Constraints | Description |
|--------|------|-------------|-------------|
| `id` | INTEGER | PRIMARY KEY, AUTOINCREMENT | Unique order identifier |
| `customerId` | INTEGER | NOT NULL, FOREIGN KEY | References `users.id` |
| `orderDate` | TEXT | NOT NULL | Order timestamp (ISO format) |
| `status` | TEXT | NOT NULL | Order status (5 possible values) |
| `totalPrice` | REAL | NOT NULL | Total order amount |

**Order Status Values** (5 statuses):

| Status | Description | Available Transitions |
|--------|-------------|----------------------|
| `Pending` | Order placed, awaiting admin confirmation | → Confirmed, → Canceled |
| [Confirmed](file:///c:/Users/Ahmed/IdeaProjects/Online-Book-Store/src/main/java/com/example/onlinebookstore/Models/OrderObserver.java#6-7) | Admin confirmed, inventory reduced (Observer) | → Shipped |
| `Shipped` | Order dispatched to customer | → Delivered |
| `Delivered` | Order completed successfully | (Terminal state) |
| `Canceled` | Order canceled by customer or admin | (Terminal state) |

**Status Flow**:
```
Pending ──→ Confirmed ──→ Shipped ──→ Delivered
   │
   └──→ Canceled
```

**Purpose**: Tracks all orders through their complete lifecycle. Status changes trigger Observer notifications.

---

### 6.2.5 Order Items Table

Stores individual items within each order.

```sql
CREATE TABLE order_items (
    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,
    orderId INTEGER NOT NULL,
    bookId INTEGER NOT NULL,
    quantity INTEGER NOT NULL,
    price REAL NOT NULL,
    FOREIGN KEY (orderId) REFERENCES orders(id),
    FOREIGN KEY (bookId) REFERENCES books(id)
);
```

**Columns**:

| Column | Type | Constraints | Description |
|--------|------|-------------|-------------|
| `id` | INTEGER | PRIMARY KEY, AUTOINCREMENT | Unique order item identifier |
| `orderId` | INTEGER | NOT NULL, FOREIGN KEY | References `orders.id` |
| `bookId` | INTEGER | NOT NULL, FOREIGN KEY | References `books.id` |
| `quantity` | INTEGER | NOT NULL | Number of books ordered |
| `price` | REAL | NOT NULL | Price per book at time of order |

**Purpose**: Details of books in each order. Allows orders to contain multiple different books.

**Design Note**: Stores `price` at time of purchase (not a reference to current book price), preserving historical pricing.

---

### 6.2.6 Reviews Table

Stores customer reviews and ratings for books.

```sql
CREATE TABLE reviews (
    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,
    bookId INTEGER NOT NULL,
    customerId INTEGER NOT NULL,
    rating INTEGER NOT NULL,  -- 1-5 stars
    comment TEXT,
    reviewDate TEXT NOT NULL,
    FOREIGN KEY (bookId) REFERENCES books(id),
    FOREIGN KEY (customerId) REFERENCES users(id)
);
```

**Columns**:

| Column | Type | Constraints | Description |
|--------|------|-------------|-------------|
| `id` | INTEGER | PRIMARY KEY, AUTOINCREMENT | Unique review identifier |
| `bookId` | INTEGER | NOT NULL, FOREIGN KEY | References `books.id` |
| `customerId` | INTEGER | NOT NULL, FOREIGN KEY | References `users.id` |
| `rating` | INTEGER | NOT NULL | Star rating (1-5) |
| `comment` | TEXT | NULL | Optional review text |
| `reviewDate` | TEXT | NOT NULL | Review timestamp (ISO format) |

**Purpose**: Allows customers to rate and review books they've purchased.

---

## 6.3 Entity Relationship Diagram

### Relationships

```
users (1) ──────< (N) orders
    Users can have multiple orders

users (1) ──────< (N) reviews
    Users can write multiple reviews

books (1) ──────< (N) order_items
    Books can appear in multiple orders

books (1) ──────< (N) reviews
    Books can have multiple reviews

orders (1) ──────< (N) order_items
    Orders contain multiple items

categories (1) ──────< (N) books
    Categories contain multiple books (via TEXT reference)
```

